Step 1: Force Structured Output

Don't ask:

Generate architecture documentation.

Ask:

Return valid JSON only.

Schema:

{
  "executive_summary":"",
  "architecture_style":"",
  "business_capabilities":[],
  "components":[],
  "external_systems":[],
  "risks":[],
  "recommendations":[]
}

Example response:

{
  "executive_summary":
    "Payment processing application",

  "architecture_style":
    "Layered Monolith",

  "business_capabilities":[
    "Payment Processing",
    "Settlement"
  ],

  "components":[
    {
      "name":"PaymentService",
      "purpose":"Handles payment lifecycle"
    }
  ],

  "external_systems":[
    "Oracle",
    "Kafka"
  ],

  "risks":[
    "Large service classes"
  ],

  "recommendations":[
    "Refactor services"
  ]
}

This makes downstream processing predictable.

Step 2: Create Document Models

Example:

from pydantic import BaseModel

class ArchitectureDocument(BaseModel):

    executive_summary: str

    architecture_style: str

    business_capabilities: list[str]

    components: list

    risks: list[str]

    recommendations: list[str]

Deserialize:

doc = ArchitectureDocument.model_validate_json(
    llm_response
)

Now you have a strongly typed document.

Step 3: Generate Professional Templates

Use Jinja2.

Example:

<h1>Architecture Overview</h1>

<p>{{ executive_summary }}</p>

<h2>Architecture Style</h2>

<p>{{ architecture_style }}</p>

<h2>Business Capabilities</h2>

<ul>
{% for capability in business_capabilities %}
<li>{{ capability }}</li>
{% endfor %}
</ul>
